anything after the store opens throws, the store is closed\n193:  before the error propagates. This gives `q` one uniform opened-index value.\n198:  rows: history views, `as-of` bounds, and clauses the index route cannot\n200:  clause (no bound e/a/v) is a third path: it stays on the index route but\n210:## Index realization\n218:storage boundary; all strategies answer identically, the index being the same\n222:their order, persistence, or realization changes query cost, not associative\n236:  reader opens a published index into a d5 relation it owns; query composition\n284:  Entity-map source normalization is a separate query-boundary convenience\n290:bound (the clause-level index route has no as-of plumbing; `bound-datoms`\n291:applies the bound at the relation level), and for any query whose clauses\n299:- **Unselective scans do more CPU work lazily.** A clause with no bound\n306:  closed store: real backends throw (`\"missing index segment\"` when a get\n309:  silently empty. Because `q` opens and closes nothing, the extent in which an\n310:  opened index is usable is the caller's to bound: it opens before the query\n322:- `(current source)` bounds by `as-of`, resolves the greatest `(t,m)` for\n324:- `(history source)` exposes the exact d5 relation, optionally bounded by\n344:  the outer scope, and every one of them must be bound there (informative\n345:  throw otherwise — an unbound var would act as a wildcard and silently negate\n349:  and those join vars must be bound. Negation forms nest (note `(not\n368:  governed/confined callers. Unknown fn, unbound argument, more than one\n376:- **Rules (recursion)** — Datomic syntax: a rule set bound to `%` via\n380:  locals; head vars must be bound by the body (throw otherwise). Recursion\n383:  cycle also has a finite acyclic derivation. Required-bound head vars\n391:stay in source position, so their vars are bound by the clauses the query\n409:  bound the walk by construction.\n424:bounded, and a served view is always a faithful projection of some prefix of\n435:- **Expose how: no new storage protocol.** Everything the index realization\n446:  published index descriptor names a manifest by content address; each read resolves\n460:  upstream plumbing and `dao.jing` staying the dumb boundary; unifying them\n462:- **Bounded realizations are values, not streams.** The DaoStream reader\n464:  append-only, retained sequence *over time*, so a descriptor can name it\n465:  across a serialization boundary, and so eviction is reported as a gap. A row\n472:- **The relation transport is eliminated.** The v1\n473:  `:dao.stream/relation` descriptor carried its tuples *inside itself* and\n474:  \"opened\" into a record that read them back by index. A descriptor names a\n475:  stream; a descriptor that *is* the data is not reachability data, it is a\n489:  those positions. The bounded tuples, their exact bound, and their explicitly\n     1\t# dao.space.query — The Reader-Side Index Consumer\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented, on DaoStream v2. Every database input is a **value**:\n     4\ta relation value (`query/relation`, `query/entity-map-relation`), a datom\n     5\tview over one (`current`, `history`), or a published covered index the caller\n     6\topened (`open-published!`). `q` opens nothing and closes nothing. A live\n     7\tDaoStream enters only through `snapshot`, the single interpreter over\n     8\t`dao.stream.observe/step`. The index realization is owned by\n     9\t`dao.space.index`, and `match` / `q` / `pull` run over one evaluator on every\n    10\tplatform, returning a local result value that `collect` materializes. This\n    11\tdocument records the read model, the source model, the index-realization\n    12\tdecision, the query surface, and the open items. The executable contract is\n    13\t`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`.\n    14\t\n    15\t**Related documents:**\n    16\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the transactor-side realization this\n    17\t  library consumes\n    18\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space; *The Query Library* and\n    19\t  *Source Polymorphism*\n    20\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary; *Physical intake versus\n    21\t  semantic composition*\n    22\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the distributed backend a published source\n    23\t  may be read through\n    24\t- `docs/design/adr/0001-dao-space-as-storage-boundary.md` — the storage\n    25\t  boundary decision and the monoid-homomorphism proof below\n    26\t- `docs/datomic.md` — the Datomic architecture the Peer model maps to\n    27\t\n    28\t## Architecture\n    29\t\n    30\tThe query library is an embeddable Peer over finite relations of tuples. It\n    31\tconsumes the logical tuples of each value it is handed and runs matching /\n    32\tDatalog / pull above them. An opened published index resolves its content-store\n    33\tcoordinate and manifest address below this boundary; the caller opens it and\n    34\tthe caller closes it. The query library is pure and stateless — it owns no\n    35\tdurable state, never writes, opens nothing, and enforces no schema; any schema\n    36\tpolicy belongs on the write side.\n    37\t\n    38\t```\n    39\tdao.space.query/q …/match …/pull         ← the TUPLE SPACE reads\n    40\t     │  consumes finite relation values and opened indexes\n    41\t     ▼\n    42\tdao.jing content store (content handles) ← STORAGE boundary\n    43\t     ▲\n    44\tdao.space.index/publish-index!  (write side, a separate library)\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\t## The read coordinate: a serializable name, opened by the caller\n    48\t\n    49\tA published covered index is named by a **serializable coordinate** whose\n    50\tlogical elements are canonical d5, with physical B-tree segments hidden below\n    51\tit. The coordinate contains a resolvable content-store coordinate plus manifest\n    52\taddress, never a live handle; its portability is conditional on that coordinate\n    53\tbeing resolvable by the receiving runtime. It is data, and may travel through a\n    54\tstream like any other data.\n    55\t\n    56\t```clojure\n    57\t(index/published-index\n    58\t  {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file\n    59\t   :path \"/srv/datom-world/content.log\"}\n    60\t  :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>)\n    61\t\n    62\t;; The resulting coordinate has this exact data shape. The\n    63\t;; :dao.stream/* keys are dao.space.index's, kept so a published index\n    64\t;; stays recognizable to index's own code; query dispatches on neither.\n    65\t{:dao.stream/type :dao.space.index/published\n    66\t :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>}\n    67\t :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt\n    68\t :content-store {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file\n    69\t                 :path \"/srv/datom-world/content.log\"}\n    70\t :manifest-address :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>}\n    71\t```\n    72\t\n    73\tA query may receive several opened indexes as separate `:in` database values.\n    74\tThe coordinate is itself data and may be transported through a stream, then\n    75\topened by whoever receives it. Source identity is interpreter context and never\n    76\ta tuple slot. It is never inferred from a DaoJing intake stream, and **a bare\n    77\tcontent-store handle carries no source**: it names no manifest, so\n    78\t`q`/`match`/`pull` reject it like any other loose value.\n    79\t\n    80\tTwo kinds of pools must not be confused:\n    81\t\n    82\t- **DaoJing intake pools** are physical ingestion topology — which streams an\n    83\t  observer materializes into content storage.\n    84\t- **Query db-values** are semantic composition — which bounded streams a\n    85\t  reader names independently in `:in`.\n    86\t\n    87\tThey never need to coincide, and DaoJing never learns the latter. A manifest\n    88\taddress is a content address like any other; it is a snapshot-at-read of an\n    89\timmutable value, not a mutable \"root\" reference, and the query layer treats it\n    90\tas such.\n    91\t\n    92\t## Source polymorphism\n    93\t\n    94\t`dao.space.query/q` accepts database values only as:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. A **relation value** — `(query/relation tuples)` or\n    97\t   `(query/entity-map-relation maps)`.\n    98\t2. A **datom view** over one — `(current src)` / `(history src)`.\n    99\t3. An **opened published index** — `(query/open-published! coordinate)`.\n   100\t\n   101\tRaw maps and vectors are not `q` database inputs, and neither is anything else\n   102\tloose: an unrecognized value throws before evaluation, naming what is accepted.\n   103\tCallers explicitly wrap arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuples with\n   104\t`query/relation` and entity maps with `query/entity-map-relation`.\n   105\t\n   106\tEach database input is one logical relation:\n   107\t\n   108\t- **A published index** — the caller opens the serializable coordinate with\n   109\t  `open-published!`, holds the content-store handle, and releases it with\n   110\t  `close-published!`. `q` borrows it and never closes it. There is no\n   111\t  registry and no dispatch on a transport type: opening a coordinate is\n   112\t  `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` plus a manifest read, a function call the caller\n   113\t  makes, not a stream operation.\n   114\t- **An explicit datom view** — `current` and `history` are semantic view\n   115\t  values interpreted by `q`/`match`/`pull`; their nested source is a relation\n   116\t  value, a snapshot's relation, or an opened published index.\n   117\t  `(current source)` resolves a canonical d5 history and projects it to d3;\n   118\t  `(history source)` preserves exact d5. Independent physical sources stay\n   119\t  separate database inputs.\n   120\t- **A wrapped relation** — `(query/relation [[...] ...])` wrapping arbitrary\n   121\t  and mixed arities. Arity never selects an interpretation.\n   122\t- **A wrapped entity-map collection** —\n   123\t  `(query/entity-map-relation [{:db/id e, :work/status :todo, ...} ...])`.\n   124\t  Normalized to datoms first: each `k v` pair becomes an `[e k v]` fact.\n   125\t  Identity is explicit: the read side is pure and never mints an entity id, so\n   126\t  every entity map must carry `:db/id`, and a map without one throws an\n   127\t  informative `ex-info` rather than inventing a tempid.\n   128\t\n   129\t`q` consumes each input exactly as far as evaluation requires, and **opens and\n   130\tcloses nothing** — it is a pure function of its arguments. A `current` view\n   131\t(`as-of` nil) over an opened published index is realized **lazily**: the\n   132\tcovered sets are re-attached as restored B-trees and only the slices the plan\n   133\ttouches are fetched; every other input — history views, `as-of` bounds,\n   134\tin-process relations — is consumed eagerly before returning. It performs\n   135\tarbitrary-dimensional exact positional matching/unification (with explicit `&`\n   136\ttail syntax) and returns a local **result value** carrying the find spec.\n   137\t`query/collect` materializes conventional Datalog shapes from it.\n   138\t\n   139\tRelation values, view values and result values are values, not transports.\n   140\tThey carry no `:dao.stream/type` and no bound: a finite collection computed in\n   141\tfull has no second observer, no future appends and no eviction, so nothing\n   142\tabout the DaoStream reader surface would describe it (see *Decisions*). A\n   143\tresult in particular is not serializable or reopenable; to transport one,\n   144\tmaterialize it with `query/collect` and wrap the retained tuples explicitly\n   145\twith `query/relation`.\n   146\t\n   147\tSource polymorphism is an ergonomic property of the query *function*, not a\n   148\tsecond medium — a local value is by definition not shared, and coordination\n   149\tbetween agents still runs through shared content storage.\n   150\t\n   151\t## Snapshots: the one stream interpreter\n   152\t\n   153\tQuery never reads a live stream during evaluation. A DaoStream v2 reader\n   154\tbecomes a query input only by an explicit snapshot, and `snapshot` is the only\n   155\tfunction in this namespace that requires `dao.stream`:\n   156\t\n   157\t```clojure\n   158\t(query/snapshot handle)\n   159\t;; => {:relation {:dao.space.query/relation [v …]}\n   160\t;;     :status   :ended | :blocked | :gap | :defect\n   161\t;;     :cursor   c            ; the cursor reached; on :gap the last retained\n   162\t;;     :recovery c'           ; :gap only\n   163\t;;     :read     raw}         ; :defect only\n   164\t```\n   165\t\n   166\tIt mints at `:dao.stream/oldest` and loops `dao.stream.observe/step` with a\n   167\ttotal effect — retain the value, answer `ok` — until the step stops.\n   168\t\n   169\t- **Every stopping outcome is data, never an exception.** `:ended` is a closed\n   170\t  stream fully read; `:blocked` is an open stream caught up, which is a\n   171\t  snapshot at call time; `:gap` carries the values read before the hole and the\n   172\t  recovery cursor; `:defect` carries the raw answer. The caller decides whether\n   173\t  a partial snapshot is a relation it can query — query has no basis to decide\n   174\t  that for it.\n   175\t- **A cursor never advances past a value the snapshot did not retain**, which\n   176\t  is `step`'s effect-before-commit discipline, and `snapshot` never closes the\n   177\t  handle.\n   178\t\n   179\t`snapshot` stays here until a second consumer needs it, at which point it\n   180\tbelongs in `dao.stream.observe` as `drain`.\n   181\t\n   182\t## Reading a manifest\n   183\t\n   184\tThe caller opens a coordinate with `query/open-published!` and releases it with\n   185\t`query/close-published!`; a second close is a no-op.\n   186\t\n   187\t- **`open-published!`** validates the coordinate against\n   188\t  `index/published-index`, opens the declared DaoJing content store, validates\n   189\t  the manifest address, fetches only the manifest, and re-attaches the four\n   190\t  covered sets lazily through `index/restored-indexes` (zero node fetches at\n   191\t  open). The EAVT row vector is deferred behind a delay and forced only by row\n   192\t  consumers. If anything after the store opens throws, the store is closed\n   193\t  before the error propagates. This gives `q` one uniform opened-index value.\n   194\t- **The engine consumes those sets directly for selective reads.** A `current`\n   195\t  view with `as-of` nil routes `::fact-index` to the restored sets, so\n   196\t  3-fixed clauses slice through `subseq-from` and fault only the seek path\n   197\t  plus the matching range. What falls back to the eager drain of the deferred\n   198\t  rows: history views, `as-of` bounds, and clauses the index route cannot\n   199\t  serve — rest patterns and 4+-slot patterns. An *unselective* 3-fixed\n   200\t  clause (no bound e/a/v) is a third path: it stays on the index route but\n   201\t  takes `select-by-index`'s `(seq (:eavt idx))` fallback, a full walk of the\n   202\t  restored tree resolved on the fly — same fetches as a drain, no\n   203\t  pre-resolution pass. `read-datoms` remains the explicit eager path.\n   204\t\n   205\t`index/read-manifest` validates before reading: the value at the manifest\n   206\taddress must be exactly `{:indexes {:eavt … :aevt … :avet … :vaet …} :count n\n   207\t:branching-factor n}` and must hash to the requested address. A query therefore\n   208\tnever interprets a foreign value as an index.\n   209\t\n   210\t## Index realization\n   211\t\n   212\t`dao.jing` exposes only the substrate an index is built from — immutable,\n   213\tcontent-addressed segments of opaque payloads — and knows nothing of indexes.\n   214\tThe *index*\n   215\titself, a covered B-Tree (EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET) a query can traverse and answer\n   216\tfrom, is the interpretation this library projects onto those payloads. How a\n   217\treader builds and maintains it is a pure performance choice made *above* the\n   218\tstorage boundary; all strategies answer identically, the index being the same\n   219\tset of datoms (ADR 0001's monoid homomorphism):\n   220\t\n   221\tThese indexes are access paths inside the DaoSpace tuple-space point. Changing\n   222\ttheir order, persistence, or realization changes query cost, not associative\n   223\tmatching semantics and not DaoSpace's position in Datom.world's moduli space of\n   224\tdatabase interpreters.\n   225\t\n   226\t- **Rebuild per query** — each read folds the source's datoms into a fresh\n   227\t  index and discards it. Simple; O(total datoms) per read. The permanent\n   228\t  fallback for small sources and `as-of` reads.\n   229\t- **Incremental index** — a long-lived reader keeps a cursor per source\n   230\t  stream and folds only new frames as they arrive (Datomic-peer style). More\n   231\t  machinery; amortized reads. Still no transactor and no global clock — each\n   232\t  stream advances its own cursor.\n   233\t- **Owner-built, peers compose (implemented)** — each stream's owner indexes\n   234\t  its own stream (`dao.space.index/publish-index!`) and persists the segments;\n   235\t  readers consume explicitly supplied coordinates as independent db-values. A\n   236\t  reader opens a published index into a d5 relation it owns; query composition\n   237\t  stays in the interpreter. Index-once, reuse-by-many, and available\n   238\t  when the author is offline — the decentralized analog of Datomic's\n   239\t  transactor-built index. Implemented on JVM,\n   240\t  ClojureScript, and ClojureDart on `dao.data.btree` (see\n   241\t  `dao.space.index.md`). `open-published!` opens lazily and `current`\n   242\t  (as-of nil) selective reads run on the restored trees; eager walks serve\n   243\t  `history`, `as-of`, and full scans.\n   244\t\n   245\t**Planning never forces the deferred relation.** The clause-cost estimator\n   246\treads a relation's truthiness without forcing it, and a deferred relation is\n   247\talways truthy, so a multi-clause query plans without draining its source and\n   248\teach clause faults only the slices it touches. This is what makes the lazy\n   249\troute exist for multi-clause queries at all, rather than being a property of\n   250\tsingle-clause reads.\n   251\t\n   252\t## The `read-datoms` contract\n   253\t\n   254\tThe eager read path is `index/read-datoms`, the function that turns what\n   255\t`jing/get` returns into the `[e a v t m]` vectors the index is built from:\n   256\t\n   257\t```clojure\n   258\t(defn read-datoms [content-store manifest-address]\n   259\t  (let [manifest (read-manifest content-store manifest-address)]\n   260\t    (vec (walk-index-datoms content-store (:eavt (:indexes manifest))))))\n   261\t```\n   262\t\n   263\tIt is a pure data-structure walk over whatever `jing/get` returns — it never\n   264\ttouches bytes or calls `edn/read-string` itself. `jing/get`'s contract is\n   265\t\"returns the stored opaque value at a strict segment address, already\n   266\tdecoded.\" Whether that value arrived via an EDN round-trip (`dao.jing.file`), a\n   267\tplain in-memory reference (`dao.jing.mem`), an RPC (`dao.jing.remote`), or a\n   268\tDHT fetch\n   269\t(`dao.jing.dht`) is backend-internal and invisible here. This is exactly the\n   270\tseam Datomic's Peer occupies internally when it decodes storage's index blobs\n   271\tbefore building indexes from them — `dao.jing` itself never decodes meaning.\n   272\t\n   273\t**Current contract:**\n   274\t\n   275\t- **Input.** A single `jing/get` of the manifest address, returning an\n   276\t  already-decoded, validated manifest.\n   277\t- **Extract.** `walk-index-datoms` reads the `:eavt` node graph eagerly, one\n   278\t  `jing/get` per node (plain EDN leaf/branch blobs), and returns the flat\n   279\t  datoms seq in index order. A nil address (an empty index) walks to ().\n   280\t- **Canonical d5 only.** Local streams and covered indexes store exactly\n   281\t  `[e a v t m]`. `read-datoms` preserves those vectors. Source scope is held\n   282\t  by the query interpreter and never stamped into a tuple.\n   283\t- **Tuple preservation.** `read-datoms` returns stored vectors unchanged.\n   284\t  Entity-map source normalization is a separate query-boundary convenience\n   285\t  that supplies `t = 0` and `m = dao.datom/default-op` (*Source\n   286\t  polymorphism*).\n   287\t\n   288\t`open-published!` opens lazily (manifest only) and the engine uses the restored\n   289\tsets directly for `current` (as-of nil) selective reads. This eager path remains the contract for `history`, for every `as-of`\n   290\tbound (the clause-level index route has no as-of plumbing; `bound-datoms`\n   291\tapplies the bound at the relation level), and for any query whose clauses\n   292\tgenuinely scan. Two observable consequences of the lazy route:\n   293\t\n   294\t- **t/m provenance.** The eager in-memory fact index rebuilds rows with\n   295\t  synthetic `t = 0` and `m = dao.datom/default-op`; the restored sets carry the\n   296\t  real `t`/`m`. The public `datoms` selector exposes the difference. Clause\n   297\t  evaluation projects `[e a v]`, so `q`/`pull`/`get-else` results are\n   298\t  unaffected; parity tests pin the `[e a v]` projections.\n   299\t- **Unselective scans do more CPU work lazily.** A clause with no bound\n   300\t  e/a/v walks the full d5 history through the `(seq (:eavt idx))` fallback and\n   301\t  resolves current state on the fly, where the eager path scans pre-resolved\n   302\t  current-only facts. Same fetches, more per-row work — a cost note, not a\n   303\t  correctness difference.\n   304\t- **After close, loudness is the backend's — and closing is the caller's.** A\n   305\t  deferred row seq faulted after `close-published!` has run reads through the\n   306\t  closed store: real backends throw (`\"missing index segment\"` when a get\n   307\t  returns the absent sentinel, or the backend's own read error); a backend\n   308\t  whose close is a no-op (an in-memory test handle) keeps serving. Never\n   309\t  silently empty. Because `q` opens and closes nothing, the extent in which an\n   310\t  opened index is usable is the caller's to bound: it opens before the query\n   311\t  and closes after it is done with the results.\n   312\t- **Planning never forces.** The clause-cost estimator reads the relation's\n   313\t  truthiness without forcing it; a deferred relation is always truthy, so\n   314\t  multi-clause queries plan without draining the source and each clause\n   315\t  faults only the slices it touches (pinned by the multi-clause budget test).\n   316\t\n   317\t## Current-state resolution\n   318\t\n   319\tThe log is append-only, so a retraction is another d5 datom. Interpretation\n   320\tis explicit:\n   321\t\n   322\t- `(current source)` bounds by `as-of`, resolves the greatest `(t,m)` for\n   323\t  each local `[e a v]`, removes retractions, and projects to d3.\n   324\t- `(history source)` exposes the exact d5 relation, optionally bounded by\n   325\t  `as-of`.\n   326\t- A bare vector relation has no temporal semantics, even when every row has\n   327\t  five positions.\n   328\t\n   329\t`as-of` is accepted only with an explicit datom view. There is no implicit\n   330\tcardinality-one schema or automatic supersession.\n   331\t\n   332\t## Datalog surface\n   333\t\n   334\t`q` implements Datalog over one or more immutable relations:\n   335\t\n   336\t- **Positive-conjunction pattern clauses** — arbitrary mixed-dimensional\n   337\t  tuples. A plain clause matches exact arity. `[fixed ... & _]` explicitly\n   338\t  ignores the remaining positions; `[fixed ... & ?tail]` binds them as a\n   339\t  vector. `match` uses the same positional contract.\n   340\t- **Negation** — `(not clause ...)` and `(not-join [?join-var ...] clause\n   341\t  ...)`. Stratified, evaluated as a failed sub-query per candidate binding\n   342\t  over the already current-state-resolved index, so retractions and\n   343\t  supersessions are honored. Plain `not` unifies every var it contains with\n   344\t  the outer scope, and every one of them must be bound there (informative\n   345\t  throw otherwise — an unbound var would act as a wildcard and silently negate\n   346\t  everything), except vars scoped to a nested `not-join`, which contributes\n   347\t  only its join vars to the requirement; `not-join`'s vector names the *only*\n   348\t  vars that unify — any other inner var is fresh even under a colliding name —\n   349\t  and those join vars must be bound. Negation forms nest (note `(not\n   350\t  (not-join ...))` is double negation: \"exists\").\n   351\t- **Disjunction** — `or` (every branch must bind the same free-var set) and\n   352\t  `or-join` (only the declared join vars unify with the outer scope; the\n   353\t  branches run from a seed of join vars plus the sources, and branch-local\n   354\t  vars are stripped before merging; each branch must statically bind every\n   355\t  declared join var). `(and ...)` groups clauses inside a branch.\n   356\t- **Aggregates** — `count`, `count-distinct`, `sum`, `min`, `max`, `avg` in\n   357\t  `:find`, with `:with` for multiplicity. Pipeline is Datomic's: project each\n   358\t  result to the find ∪ `:with` ∪ aggregate-arg vars, dedupe those tuples as a\n   359\t  set, group by the find vars only (`:with` vars keep intended duplicates\n   360\t  within a group, never split groups), aggregate per group.\n   361\t- **Predicates & function clauses** — `[(f ?a ...)]` filters; `[(f ?a ...)\n   362\t  ?out]` (or `[?o1 ?o2]` for tuple destructuring) binds. `f` resolves from a\n   363\t  caller-supplied `{:fns {sym fn}}` entry — no symbol `resolve`, no hidden\n   364\t  global registry, so the surface stays pure and clj/cljs/cljd-portable. A\n   365\t  default `builtins` registry of pure, data-first functions (`=`, `<`,\n   366\t  arithmetic, `str`, `count`, `get`, `tuple`/`untuple`, `ground`, …) resolves\n   367\t  before caller fns and can be disabled with `{:builtins false}` for\n   368\t  governed/confined callers. Unknown fn, unbound argument, more than one\n   369\t  binding form, tuple-arity mismatch, and the collection/relation binding\n   370\t  forms (`[?y ...]`, `[[?a ?b]]` — not implemented) all throw. Predicates keep\n   371\t  a binding on any *truthy* return — broader than Datomic's boolean contract,\n   372\t  so a predicate returning `0` keeps the row where Datomic would be a type\n   373\t  error.\n   374\t- **Special forms** — `(get-else $ ?e ?a default)` and `(missing? $ ?e ?a)`\n   375\t  probe the index directly as fn clauses.\n   376\t- **Rules (recursion)** — Datomic syntax: a rule set bound to `%` via\n   377\t  `:in $ %`, invoked as `(rule-name arg ...)` clauses. Multiple definitions of\n   378\t  one head are a disjunction. A rule body runs in a fresh scope seeded from\n   379\t  the caller's resolved args, so body vars are rule-local like not-join\n   380\t  locals; head vars must be bound by the body (throw otherwise). Recursion\n   380\t  locals; head vars must be bound by the body (throw otherwise). Recursion\n   381\t  terminates on cyclic data by failing any in-progress call with identical\n   382\t  resolved args — sound for Datalog, where every fact derivable through a\n   383\t  cycle also has a finite acyclic derivation. Required-bound head vars\n   384\t  (`[(rule [?a] ?b) ...]`) are not implemented (throw).\n   385\t- **Find specs** — relation (default), scalar (`.`), tuple (`[?x]`), and\n   386\t  collection (`[?x ...]`) results, plus the return-map forms `:keys`,\n   387\t  `:syms`, `:strs` (relation-only, arity-checked).\n   388\t\n   389\tThe clause planner (`plan-where`) reorders only contiguous runs of pattern\n   390\tclauses by selectivity; negation, rule, and fn clauses are order barriers that\n   391\tstay in source position, so their vars are bound by the clauses the query\n   392\tauthor placed before them. Multi-source `:in $ $2 …` folds each source\n   393\tindependently.\n   394\t\n   395\t## Pull\n   396\t\n   397\t`pull` is the third read verb alongside `match` (template → datoms) and `q`\n   398\t(Datalog → relations): declarative entity projection, entity → tree. Its\n   399\tschema-free design rulings:\n   400\t\n   401\t- No schema: every attr is potentially multi-valued; forward attrs follow the\n   402\t  entity-attrs convention (one datom → scalar, more → vector), reverse attrs\n   403\t  (`:_attr`) always return a vector.\n   404\t- Ref-ness is asserted by the pattern, not guessed: a nested map spec\n   405\t  navigates values as entity ids.\n   406\t- Missing attrs are omitted, not nil-valued, unless the pattern gives\n   407\t  `:default`; `:db/id` is included in every result map.\n   408\t- Recursion markers (`'...` / depth limits) are deferred: finite patterns\n   409\t  bound the walk by construction.\n   410\t- `(pull ?e pattern)` also works as a `:find` element, resolved against the\n   411\t  already-folded index. `pull-many` shares one fold; `entity-attrs` is the\n   412\t  flat wildcard convenience.\n   413\t\n   414\t## Freshness\n   415\t\n   416\tCalling the persisted indexes materialized views forces an explicit answer:\n   417\t\n   418\t- A fold of a raw source always reads canonical truth (the datoms directly).\n   419\t- A served manifest can lag the current tail of its stream — it is a\n   420\t  precomputed positional index naming an indexed snapshot the owner published.\n   421\t\n   422\tIn an append-only world this is benign: indexes only *grow* as datoms are\n   423\tadded (retractions are semantic, above storage), so lag is monotone and\n   424\tbounded, and a served view is always a faithful projection of some prefix of\n   425\tthe log. Because any reader can reproduce a served view by folding the\n   426\tcanonical datoms, a served view stays \"acceleration of the one medium,\" not a\n   427\tsecond coordinating substrate. A caller wanting fresher data has the owner\n   428\trepublish and folds the new manifest; there is no promise beyond that.\n   429\t\n   430\t## Decisions\n   431\t\n   432\tThe following calls were settled in the 2026-07-09 discussion and remain in\n   433\tforce:\n   434\t\n   435\t- **Expose how: no new storage protocol.** Everything the index realization\n   436\t  needs is built on `dao.jing/materialize!` and `dao.jing/get` over strict\n   437\t  segment addresses (see `dao.space.index.md`). Immutable B-tree nodes are\n   438\t  just more content-addressed blobs; lazy traversal, when an interpreter uses\n   439\t  `restored-indexes`, is a **reader-side** property — the tree pulls a node only when traversal reaches it, and storage\n   440\t  never scans or seeks, it just answers `get`. Any \"storage-side\n   441\t  materialization\" would mean a backend *computing* something, which collides\n   442\t  head-on with storage-never-interprets. A backend-private network shortcut\n   443\t  (e.g. `dao.jing.dht` batching) stays possible but lives entirely inside that\n   444\t  backend's own transport.\n   445\t- **Coordinate semantics: reference at naming, snapshot at read.** A\n   446\t  published index descriptor names a manifest by content address; each read resolves\n   447\t  the immutable value at that address. This is Datomic's `d/db` pattern\n   448\t  exactly (a db value is immutable; calling `d/db` again gets a fresher one) —\n   449\t  no new mechanism, just \"immutable segments + explicit address.\"\n   450\t- **Owner-built, peers-merge is the target (implemented).** Incremental\n   451\t  indexing is the degenerate case where owner and reader coincide.\n   452\t  Rebuild-per-query stays the permanent fallback for small sources or when no\n   453\t  persisted manifest exists yet.\n   454\t- **Freshness: explicit, monotone lag.** A reader's merged view reflects\n   455\t  whatever manifest each source names *at fold time* — never live, never\n   456\t  blocking.\n   457\t- **`dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification: ruled out.** The source stays a\n   458\t  convention layered over the dumb content store. The DHT division of labor,\n   459\t  the observer design, and the tuple space all depend on `dao.stream` staying\n   460\t  upstream plumbing and `dao.jing` staying the dumb boundary; unifying them\n   461\t  would undo that.\n   462\t- **Bounded realizations are values, not streams.** The DaoStream reader\n   463\t  surface exists so independent cursors can observe one positioned,\n   464\t  append-only, retained sequence *over time*, so a descriptor can name it\n   465\t  across a serialization boundary, and so eviction is reported as a gap. A row\n   466\t  vector computed in full has none of those needs — no second observer, no\n   467\t  future appends, no eviction, no reachability. Dressing one as a reader would\n   468\t  owe a manifest, an exclusion reason for every outcome it cannot produce, and\n   469\t  the conformance harness, and would buy a `next` loop over `nth`. So\n   470\t  relations, views and results are plain tagged values, and `rows` gives a\n   471\t  view's resolved rows to a caller that wants them.\n   472\t- **The relation transport is eliminated.** The v1\n   473\t  `:dao.stream/relation` descriptor carried its tuples *inside itself* and\n   474\t  \"opened\" into a record that read them back by index. A descriptor names a\n   475\t  stream; a descriptor that *is* the data is not reachability data, it is a\n   476\t  value with a transport type stamped on it. `query/relation` returns that\n   477\t  value directly, and a relation value travels through any stream that can\n   478\t  carry data — which is all the transport ever provided.\n   479\t\n   480\t## Open items\n   481\t\n   482\t- **Published indexed snapshots and K-way merge** — several explicitly scoped\n   483\t  manifests could be exposed as an explicit derived relation without flattening\n   484\t  source identity, then answered by merging N restored B-trees in index order.\n   485\t  Passing several opened indexes as separate `:in` values is already supported;\n   486\t  what remains is merging them into one relation.\n   487\t- **Generic relation positional indexes** — a positional index orders a\n   488\t  relation by explicitly selected tuple positions without assigning meaning to\n   489\t  those positions. The bounded tuples, their exact bound, and their explicitly\n   490\t  requested positional indexes form an **indexed snapshot**. Arbitrary tuples\n   491\t  currently use a relation scan. An explicit\n   492\t  datom interpreter automatically supplies the covered indexes for canonical\n   493\t  d5 facts; arity alone never selects that interpretation. Every other n-tuple\n   494\t  relation must explicitly request its positional indexes from the caller or\n   495\t  publisher. The planner may select only from those supplied indexes and falls\n   496\t  back to a relation scan when none fits; it never constructs every positional\n   497\t  permutation implicitly. This generic path remains future work.\n   498\t- **Segment GC and incremental indexing** — see `dao.space.index.md`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The substantive P2 promotions are present: L4 is explicit under Index realization, S1–S3 are stated as properties in the new Snapshots section, ownership is caller-side, and both Decisions are recorded. I found a small amount of remaining/incorrect descriptor language, including one sentence that contradicts `open-published!`’s validation, so I’m checking whether it is isolated or systemic before giving the verdict."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"\\\\[dao\\\\.stream( |\\\\])|\\\\[dao\\\\.stream :as|dao\\\\.stream\\\\.relation|ns dao\\\\.stream\\\\.relation\\\" src/cljc/dao/space src/clj/dao/space src/cljs/dao/space test/dao/space 2>/dev/null; git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD | rg '\"'^src/.*/dao/stream/relation'\"\\\\.cljc\"'$|''^src/cljc/dao/stream/relation'\"\\\\.cljc\"'$'\"'; git status --short docs/design/dao.space.query.md docs/design/dao.stream.md; git diff --check -- docs/design/dao.space.query.md docs/design/dao.stream.md; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '140,225p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '225,280p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '330,410p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '1460,1510p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"\\\\[dao\\\\.stream( |\\\\])|\\\\[dao\\\\.stream :as|dao\\\\.stream\\\\.relation|ns dao\\\\.stream\\\\.relation\\\" src/cljc/dao/space src/clj/dao/space src/cljs/dao/space test/dao/space 2>/dev/null; git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD | rg '\"'^src/.*/dao/stream/relation'\"\\\\.cljc\"'$|''^src/cljc/dao/stream/relation'\"\\\\.cljc\"'$'\"'; git status --short docs/design/dao.space.query.md docs/design/dao.stream.md; git diff --check -- docs/design/dao.space.query.md docs/design/dao.stream.md; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '140,225p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '225,280p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '330,410p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '1460,1510p'\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:44:            [dao.stream :as ds]\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:46:  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\ntest/dao/space/schema_test.cljc:17:            [dao.stream :as ds]\ntest/dao/space/schema_test.cljc:21:  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:19:            [dao.stream :as ds]\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:24:  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\ntest/dao/space/schema_fixtures.cljc:10:  (:require [dao.stream :as ds])\ntest/dao/space/schema_fixtures.cljc:11:  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\ntest/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:31:            [dao.stream :as ds]\ntest/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc:15:            [dao.stream :as ds]\ntest/dao/space/index_test.cljc:19:            [dao.stream :as ds]\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc:18:            [dao.stream :as ds])\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/schema.cljc:19:  #?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream])))\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:40:            [dao.stream :as ds]\n M docs/design/dao.space.query.md\n M docs/design/dao.stream.md\n   140\t;; =============================================================================\n   141\t\n   142\t(defn value?\n   143\t  \"True for the query values q/match/pull take as database inputs: a\n   144\t   relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index. Raw vectors\n   145\t   and raw maps answer false.\"\n   146\t  [x]\n   147\t  (and (map? x)\n   148\t       (or (contains? x :dao.space.query/relation)\n   149\t           (contains? x :dao.space.query/view)\n   150\t           (contains? x :dao.space.query/published))))\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t(defn relation\n   154\t  \"An in-memory relation value. Its retained contents are an arbitrary,\n   155\t   mixed-dimensional tuple collection; arity never selects an\n   156\t   interpretation. This is the replacement for the direct raw-vector input.\"\n   157\t  [tuples]\n   158\t  {:dao.space.query/relation (vec tuples)})\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\t(defn entity-map-relation\n   162\t  \"An entity-map relation value: a bounded relation of entity maps,\n   163\t   projected explicitly to [e a v] facts on the read side. The read side is\n   164\t   pure and never mints an entity id, so every map must carry :db/id.\"\n   165\t  [maps]\n   166\t  ;; Normalize at the explicit constructor boundary. The resulting value is\n   167\t  ;; the same generic relation value q consumes; no second transport type\n   168\t  ;; or opening path is needed merely because the input syntax was maps.\n   169\t  (relation (entity-maps->d3 maps)))\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\t(defn fact-relation\n   173\t  \"The explicit current-fact relation: d3 rows that are already\n   174\t   current-state facts, so the fact index is built for them without a d5\n   175\t   pass. dao.space.schema builds these directly.\"\n   176\t  [tuples]\n   177\t  (assoc (relation tuples) :fact? true))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defn- snapshot-value?\n   181\t  [x]\n   182\t  (and (map? x) (contains? x :relation) (contains? x :status)))\n   183\t\n   184\t\n   185\t(defn- db-source\n   186\t  \"Normalize a current/history source. Query values and snapshots pass\n   187\t   through (a snapshot contributes its relation); everything else — raw\n   188\t   maps and vectors included — throws.\"\n   189\t  [source]\n   190\t  (cond\n   191\t    (value? source) source\n   192\t    (snapshot-value? source) (:relation source)\n   193\t    :else\n   194\t    (throw (ex-info \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\"\n   195\t                    {:input source}))))\n   196\t\n   197\t\n   198\t(defn- view-value\n   199\t  [kind source as-of]\n   200\t  (cond-> {:dao.space.query/view kind, :source source}\n   201\t    (some? as-of) (assoc :as-of as-of)))\n   202\t\n   203\t\n   204\t(defn current\n   205\t  \"The explicit current d5 interpreter: a view value over source. Resolves\n   206\t   the greatest t per [e a v], removes retractions, and rejects conflicting\n   207\t   [e a v t] rows with differing m. An optional as-of bounds visible datoms\n   208\t   to t <= as-of.\"\n   209\t  ([source] (current source nil))\n   210\t  ([source as-of]\n   211\t   (view-value :current (db-source source) as-of)))\n   212\t\n   213\t\n   214\t(defn history\n   215\t  \"The explicit history d5 interpreter: a view value over source exposing\n   216\t   the exact logical d5 rows. An optional as-of bounds visible datoms to\n   217\t   t <= as-of.\"\n   218\t  ([source] (history source nil))\n   219\t  ([source as-of]\n   220\t   (view-value :history (db-source source) as-of)))\n   221\t\n   222\t\n   223\t;; =============================================================================\n   224\t;; Published indexes: open-published! / close-published! (Decision 1)\n   225\t;; =============================================================================\n   225\t;; =============================================================================\n   226\t\n   227\t(defn open-published!\n   228\t  \"Open a published covered-index coordinate — the serializable map\n   229\t   `index/published-index` builds — as a query value the caller owns: the\n   230\t   content-store handle it opens is closed by `close-published!`, never by\n   231\t   a query. Nothing is fetched beyond the manifest; the row vector stays\n   232\t   deferred behind :rows and the covered sets behind restored B-trees, so a\n   233\t   `current` view with no as-of can answer selective clauses without a\n   234\t   full drain.\"\n   235\t  [coordinate]\n   236\t  (when-not (and (map? coordinate)\n   237\t                 (= :dao.space.index/published (:dao.stream/type coordinate)))\n   238\t    (throw (ex-info \"open-published! takes a published-index coordinate\"\n   239\t                    {:coordinate coordinate})))\n   240\t  (let [{:keys [content-store manifest-address]} coordinate\n   241\t        expected (index/published-index content-store manifest-address)]\n   242\t    (when-not (= expected coordinate)\n   243\t      (throw (ex-info \"invalid published-index coordinate\"\n   244\t                      {:coordinate coordinate, :expected expected})))\n   245\t    (let [store (jing-coordinate/open! content-store)]\n   246\t      (try\n   247\t        (let [manifest (index/read-manifest store manifest-address)]\n   248\t          {:dao.space.query/published coordinate\n   249\t           :indexes (index/restored-indexes store manifest)\n   250\t           :rows (delay (index/read-datoms store manifest-address))\n   251\t           :store store\n   252\t           :close-guard (atom false)})\n   253\t        (catch #?(:cljd Object\n   254\t                  :clj Throwable\n   255\t                  :cljs :default)\n   256\t               error\n   257\t          (jing/close! store)\n   258\t          (throw error))))))\n   259\t\n   260\t\n   261\t(defn close-published!\n   262\t  \"Close the content-store handle an opened published index owns. A second\n   263\t   close is a no-op here, before the backend is even asked.\"\n   264\t  [opened]\n   265\t  (when-let [guard (:close-guard opened)]\n   266\t    (when (compare-and-set! guard false true)\n   267\t      (jing/close! (:store opened))))\n   268\t  nil)\n   269\t\n   270\t\n   271\t;; =============================================================================\n   272\t;; snapshot: the one dao.stream interpreter (Decision 3)\n   273\t;; =============================================================================\n   274\t\n   275\t(defn snapshot\n   276\t  \"Turn a dao.stream reader handle into a relation value:\n   277\t\n   278\t     {:relation <relation value>\n   279\t      :status   :ended | :blocked | :gap | :defect\n   280\t      :cursor   c            ; the cursor reached; :gap keeps the last retained\n   330\t  [relation]\n   331\t  (index/index-datoms (mapv (fn [tuple]\n   332\t                              [(nth tuple 0) (nth tuple 1)\n   333\t                               (nth tuple 2) 0 datom/default-op])\n   334\t                            relation)))\n   335\t\n   336\t\n   337\t(defn- force-relation\n   338\t  \"Force a relation if it is delayed (e.g. from a lazy covered-index\n   339\t   realization) or return it directly. Memoization belongs to the delay:\n   340\t   forcing is therefore at most one walk, but this function itself neither\n   341\t   creates nor guarantees non-nilness.\"\n   342\t  [relation]\n   343\t  (if (delay? relation) @relation relation))\n   344\t\n   345\t\n   346\t(declare realize-db-value!)\n   347\t\n   348\t\n   349\t(defn- realize-datom-view\n   350\t  \"Interpret a current/history view inside the query layer. The nested\n   351\t   source is another query value; a current view with no as-of over an\n   352\t   opened published index routes through the restored covered sets, every\n   353\t   other combination resolves the source's rows.\"\n   354\t  [d]\n   355\t  (let [kind (:dao.space.query/view d)\n   356\t        as-of (:as-of d)\n   357\t        {src-relation ::relation, src-indexes ::indexes}\n   358\t        (realize-db-value! (:source d))]\n   359\t    (if (and (= :current kind) (nil? as-of) src-indexes)\n   360\t      {::relation (delay (d5->current-facts (force-relation src-relation) nil)),\n   361\t       ::fact-index src-indexes}\n   362\t      (let [forced-rel (force-relation src-relation)\n   363\t            rows (case kind\n   364\t                   :current (d5->current-facts forced-rel as-of)\n   365\t                   :history (d5->history-rows forced-rel as-of))]\n   366\t        {::relation rows,\n   367\t         ::fact-index (when (= :current kind)\n   368\t                        (relation->fact-index rows))}))))\n   369\t\n   370\t\n   371\t(defn ^:no-doc realize-db-value!\n   372\t  \"Turn one db-value into an immutable tuple relation plus, for current\n   373\t   fact views, an in-memory fact index. Opens nothing, closes nothing.\"\n   374\t  [db-value]\n   375\t  (when-not (map? db-value)\n   376\t    (throw\n   377\t      (ex-info\n   378\t        \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\"\n   379\t        {:input db-value})))\n   380\t  (cond\n   381\t    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/relation)\n   382\t    (let [tuples (:dao.space.query/relation db-value)]\n   383\t      {::relation tuples,\n   384\t       ::fact-index (when (:fact? db-value) (relation->fact-index tuples))})\n   385\t\n   386\t    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/published)\n   387\t    (let [indexes (index/covered-indexes db-value)]\n   388\t      {::relation (:rows db-value),\n   389\t       ::indexes indexes,\n   390\t       ::fact-index nil})\n   391\t\n   392\t    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/view)\n   393\t    (realize-datom-view db-value)\n   394\t\n   395\t    :else\n   396\t    (throw\n   397\t      (ex-info\n   398\t        \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\"\n   399\t        {:input db-value}))))\n   400\t\n   401\t\n   402\t;; =============================================================================\n   403\t;; match: Linda-style positional template (an ergonomic materializer)\n   404\t;; =============================================================================\n   405\t\n   406\t(def ^:private FREE ::free)\n   407\t\n   408\t\n   409\t(defn- wildcard?\n   410\t  [x]\n  1460\t\n  1461\t\n  1462\t(defn collect\n  1463\t  \"Materialize a q result value into plain Clojure data: relation (set of\n  1464\t   tuples), scalar, tuple, collection, or return-map.\"\n  1465\t  [result]\n  1466\t  (when-not (and (map? result) (contains? result :dao.space.query/result))\n  1467\t    (throw (ex-info \"collect requires a query result value\" {:result result})))\n  1468\t  (let [rows (:dao.space.query/result result)\n  1469\t        spec-result (apply-spec (:spec result) rows)]\n  1470\t    (if-let [rm-key (:return-map-key result)]\n  1471\t      (apply-return-map {:return-map-key rm-key,\n  1472\t                         :return-map-keys (:return-map-keys result)}\n  1473\t                        spec-result)\n  1474\t      spec-result)))\n  1475\t\n  1476\t\n  1477\t;; =============================================================================\n  1478\t;; Entity Attributes\n  1479\t;; =============================================================================\n  1480\t\n  1481\t(defn entity-attrs\n  1482\t  \"Convenience: return a map of {attr val} for the given entity in source.\n  1483\t   If multiple datoms exist for an attribute, returns a vector of values.\"\n  1484\t  [source eid]\n  1485\t  (dissoc (pull source eid '[*]) :db/id))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
